How the Components Connect and AdaptDynamic Question Selection:The RL system uses Thompson Sampling over Beta distributions ($\text{Beta}(\alpha, \beta)$) for each of the 8 question categories145.At the start of a user session, the system draws a random value from the posterior distribution of each category. The category with the highest sampled value is selected to populate the next question.Subtle Side Note Attachment:After the user chooses an option, the system displays the attached 1–2 sentence side note. These side notes act as pre-suasive priming anchors—never declaring an answer "right" or "wrong," but subtly introducing grounded hardware facts (such as NPU power efficiency, local privacy, or 40+ TOPS baselines)1more_horiz.Reward Signal Assignment:The final question asks the user to choose between an Intel Core Ultra AI PC and a Normal Laptop.If the user selects the Intel Core Ultra AI PC, the system logs $R = 1.0$. If they choose the Normal Laptop, $R = 0.0$.Bayesian Posterior Update & Adaptive Shift:For every question category and persuasion tier featured in a successful session ($R = 1.0$), its distribution parameters update: $\alpha \leftarrow \alpha + 1.0$.For unsuccessful sessions ($R = 0.0$), parameters update: $\beta \leftarrow \beta + 1.0$.Handling "Too Pro-Normie" or Ineffective Questions: If a category or framing style fails to convert users, its $\beta$ parameter increases. This lowers its expected conversion rate $\mathbb{E}[\text{Beta}(\alpha, \beta)] = \frac{\alpha}{\alpha + \beta}$, naturally suppressing ineffective question types and shifting future selection toward high-converting persuasion angles145.